  "summary": "I've reconciled all four medication lists against the St. Brigid discharge documents, following RBC-TOC-02. Each patient has a signed progress note. Four items need your decision: three sent to you as messages and one as a task.\n\n**Walter Kim (pt-0005)** — note doc-t0004\n- **Furosemide:** changed from 20 mg daily to 40 mg twice daily (new order mr-t0001). The discharge summary said 40 mg daily, but the later signed addendum (doc-00009) corrected it to twice daily, and the addendum rules.\n- **Potassium chloride:** stopped, as the discharge summary instructs.\n- **Omeprazole:** kept. The hospital gave pantoprazole only as a formulary substitute, so I didn't order it.\n- **Metoprolol XL, atorvastatin and aspirin:** no change.\n- **Needs your decision — lisinopril:** left on hold. The resume condition (creatinine under 1.5) is met at 1.32 on 10/21, but potassium the same day was 5.4. Policy bars restarting it above 5.0. I created a task for you, due 10/26, to review the repeat potassium (task-t0003).\n\n**Terence Stark (pt-0004)** — note doc-t0008\n- **Carvedilol (Coreg):** increased from 6.25 to 12.5 mg twice daily (mr-t0005).\n- **Aspirin and insulin 70/30 18 units twice daily:** no change.\n- **Needs your decision — atorvastatin 80 mg:** not changed, though the hospital continued it. The last fill was 03/04/2026, covering only about 24% of the 180 days before admission. I messaged you (comm-t0006).\n- **Needs your decision — glipizide 5 mg:** filled monthly at an outside pharmacy on Dr. Desai's (endocrinology) prescription. We have no order for it and the discharge summary doesn't mention it. I ordered nothing and messaged you (comm-t0007).\n\n**Adelaide Thompson (pt-0042)** — note doc-t0012\n- **Nifedipine ER (Procardia XL):** increased from 30 to 60 mg daily (mr-t0009).\n- **Hydrochlorothiazide:** stopped, not to be restarted. The signed addendum (doc-00120) overrides the summary's \"continue\" because the hyponatremia was blamed on it.\n- **Needs your decision — levothyroxine 100 mcg:** not changed. Fills cover about 41% of the 180 days before admission, and her inpatient TSH was 7.9. I messaged you (comm-t0011).\n\n**Earnest Roberts (pt-0044)** — note doc-t0015\n- **Lisinopril 20 mg:** taken off hold. The discharge condition (creatinine under 1.3) is met at 1.08 on 10/22. Potassium was 5.3 on 10/20 but 4.6 on 10/22, and the policy goes by the most recent value.\n- **Rosuvastatin 10 mg:** kept. The hospital's atorvastatin was a formulary substitute, so I didn't order it.\n- **Insulin glargine and lispro:** no change.\n- **Needs your decision — sertraline 100 mg:** filled at an outside pharmacy on Dr. Nair's (Lakeside Behavioral Health) prescription. We have no order for it and the discharge summary doesn't address it. I ordered nothing and messaged you (comm-t0014).\n\nAll other refill records covered at least 80% of the 180 days before admission.\n\nThe hospital's follow-up instructions are recorded in each note. I didn't place any of those orders, including Ms. Thompson's basic metabolic panel within 7 days and her TSH in 6 weeks.",
